                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “You don’t know nothing about the bullpen, Wilson!”
   “I know the bullpen better than your mother’s cooking!”
   The kid in the chair was laughing so hard Papo had to set the clip‐
pers down.
   On the wall by the register, hanging by its cord from a nail, was a
small white speaker with an iris decal. Under it, in Sharpie on a paper
plate: RIP.
   “Mira quién volvió,” said Papo, without turning from the argument.
   I sat in the waiting chair with my left ear to the room and listened to
a barbershop be loud. I’d have said I wasn’t going to cry. Wilson
handed me a tissue without looking at me.
   “Allergies,” said Wilson. “It’s the clippers.”



Mama Tete got her key.
   There was no showdown, only a subpoena, served at the Armory’s
lower level on a Tuesday morning to a manager whose lawyers had
told him to be extremely helpful.
   “I still want the key,” she told Sofía.
   “You have a subpoena. A key isn’t—”
   “I said I want the key.”
   The manager, who would have given her the building, took a ring
from his belt, found the right brass, and set it in her palm. Mama Tete
said nothing. She closed her fist. Sofía opened her mouth and shut it.
   “You’re welcome,” said Sofía.
   “I said nothing,” said Mama Tete.
   The annex smelled like a library nobody was allowed to enter: steel
shelves, flat gray boxes, shellac discs in paper sleeves, each with a word
stamped in the corner in red.
   Retenido.
   It took her forty minutes to find the shelf. When she put her hands
on the box she stood very still.
   Janvier. 1925. Six sides.




